.TH WHICH 1 2026-05-12 "Substrate" "Substrate User Commands"
.SH NAME
which \- locate a program file in the user's PATH
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B which
.RI [ options ]
.IR name ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
For each
.I name
on the command line,
.B which
searches the directories listed in the
.B PATH
environment variable, in order, and prints the absolute path of the
first executable file matching that name.
.PP
With
.B \-a
(or
.BR \-\-all ),
all matches are printed instead of just the first.
.PP
If a
.I name
contains a slash, no
.B PATH
search is performed and the
.I name
itself is checked for executability.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-all
Print every matching pathname in
.B PATH
order, not just the first.
.TP
.B \-s
Silent.  Suppress all output; exit status alone indicates whether
each
.I name
was found.  BSD convention.
.TP
.B \-\-skip\-dot
Ignore
.B PATH
entries that start with a literal
.RB ` . '.
.TP
.B \-\-skip\-tilde
Ignore
.B PATH
entries that start with
.RB ` ~ '.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-dot
When a match is in the current working directory, print it as
.IB ./ name
instead of expanding to the full
.B $PWD
path.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-tilde
When a match is in
.BR $HOME ,
print it as
.IB ~/ relative
instead of expanding to the full
.B $HOME
path.
.TP
.B \-\-tty\-only
Apply
.B \-\-show\-dot
and
.B \-\-show\-tilde
transformations only when standard output is a terminal.  Useful when
output is piped to another program that wants absolute paths.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-read\-alias
GNU compatibility; not implemented on Substrate.  Substrate's
.B which
runs as a standalone program and has no integration with the shell's
alias table, so this flag is accepted with a one-line warning to
standard error.
.TP
.B \-\-skip\-alias
No-op (we don't read aliases in the first place).
.TP
.B \-\-read\-functions
GNU compatibility; not implemented on Substrate.  Warned and ignored.
.TP
.B \-\-skip\-functions
No-op.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the program version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
Every
.I name
was found.
.TP
.B 1
One or more
.I name
arguments could not be located in
.BR PATH .
.TP
.B 2
Usage error (unknown option, no
.I name
arguments).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PATH
Colon-separated list of directories to search.  An empty component
is treated as the current directory.
.TP
.B HOME
Used by
.B \-\-show\-tilde
to recognise paths that should be abbreviated with
.RB ` ~ '.
.SH EXAMPLES
Locate the
.I gcc
binary that will be invoked when the user types
.RI ` gcc ':
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ which gcc
/usr/bin/gcc
.fi
.RE
.PP
Show every
.I as
in
.B PATH
(useful when binutils and a homebrew toolchain coexist):
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ which -a as
/usr/bin/as
/usr/local/bin/as
.fi
.RE
.PP
Silent existence probe in a shell script:
.PP
.RS
.nf
if which -s gmake; then
        gmake
else
        make
fi
.fi
.RE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR command (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR access (2),
.BR execvp (3),
.BR environ (7)
.SH STANDARDS
POSIX does not standardise
.BR which ;
the POSIX-recommended portable equivalent is
.IR "command -v" .
This implementation merges the BSD
.RB ( \-a ", " \-s )
and GNU
.RB ( \-\-all ,
.BR \-\-skip\-dot ,
.B \-\-show\-tilde
and the rest)
option sets into a single binary so the same
.B which
invocation works on either flavour.
.SH HISTORY
.B which
appeared in Berkeley csh, was rewritten as a standalone C program in
4.3BSD-Reno, and has been re-implemented and extended many times since.
This Substrate implementation was written from scratch and patterned
on the
.BR id (1)
single-binary-multi-flavour approach.
